<section class="section-block">
	<h2>Before you start</h2>
	<ol>
		<li><strong>A VoiceML account with a phone number.</strong> See <a href="/voiceml/">VoiceML</a> or <a href="/signup/">start a free trial</a>.</li>
		<li><strong>Speech recognition.</strong> Nothing to set up for English: accounts without their own speech recognizer use the platform default, which recognizes English only. To use another engine or language, see <a href="/voiceml/speech-recognition/">speech recognition</a>.</li>
		<li><strong>The phone that should ring</strong>, and the numbers for the block and family lists, in E.164 format.</li>
	</ol>
	<div class="alert alert-warning" role="note">
		<p><strong>The numbers in these documents are fictional.</strong> They use the 555-0100 to 555-0199 range, which is set aside for fiction. Replace them with real numbers before you publish.</p>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="section-block">
	<h2>Step 1: Publish the goodbye document</h2>
	<p>Every <code>&lt;Record&gt;</code> needs an <code>action</code>; without one, the runtime requests the current document again and the caller records a second time. This document is that <code>action</code>.</p>
	<p class="code-block-header">goodbye.xml</p>
	<pre class="code-block" data-lang="xml"><code>{{ voicemlSamples["home-alone-screening"].goodbye | escape }}</code></pre>
	<p>Paste it at <a href="{{ site.voicemlPublisherUrl }}">voiceml.org</a>, validate it, and publish. It contains no Liquid, so it is served from an <code>/s/</code> URL. Keep the URL exactly as returned, <code>key</code> included. The key is a shared secret, so don&rsquo;t post the URL publicly.</p>
</section>

<section class="section-block">
	<h2>Step 2: Publish the screening document</h2>
	<p>Replace <code>GOODBYE_URL</code> with the URL from step 1 and the house number with your own, then publish.</p>
	<p class="code-block-header">screen.xml</p>
	<pre class="code-block" data-lang="xml"><code>{{ voicemlSamples["home-alone-screening"].screen | escape }}</code></pre>
	<p>It contains Liquid tags, so it is published to an <code>/r/</code> URL and rendered on every call with that call&rsquo;s parameters.</p>
</section>

<section class="section-block">
	<h2>Step 3: Publish the main document and point the number at it</h2>
	<p>Replace <code>SCREEN_URL</code> with the <code>/r/</code> URL from step 2, and fill in your block list, family list, and house number. Publish it, and set the <code>/r/</code> URL it returns as the phone number&rsquo;s voice URL.</p>
	<p class="code-block-header">main.xml</p>
	<pre class="code-block" data-lang="xml"><code>{{ voicemlSamples["home-alone-screening"].main | escape }}</code></pre>
</section>

<section class="section-block">
	<h2>Step 4: Test it</h2>
	<ol>
		<li>Call from a number on neither list and say &ldquo;This is Kate, calling about Kevin.&rdquo; The house phone rings.</li>
		<li>Call again and say &ldquo;I&rsquo;m calling about your car&rsquo;s extended warranty.&rdquo; You are asked to leave a message.</li>
		<li>Add your own number to the family list, publish again, repoint the number, and call: the house phone rings with no questions.</li>
	</ol>
</section>

<section class="section-block">
	<h2>How the screening works</h2>
	<p>The main document compares <code>From</code>, the caller&rsquo;s number in E.164, with two lists built by <code>{% raw %}{% assign blocked = "+18475550188,+18475550189" | split: "," %}{% endraw %}</code>. Liquid&rsquo;s <code>contains</code> tests whether a list holds a value, so <code>{% raw %}{% if blocked contains From %}{% endraw %}</code> is the whole check. A blocked caller gets <code>&lt;Reject reason="busy"/&gt;</code>. It comes before any other verb, so the call is refused without being answered.</p>
	<p>Everyone else hits a <code>&lt;Gather input="speech"&gt;</code> whose <code>action</code> is the screening document. That document receives what the caller said as <code>SpeechResult</code>, lower-cases it, and tests it with <code>contains</code>, in this order:</p>
	<div class="table-container">
		<table class="data-table">
			<caption>How the screening document decides</caption>
			<thead>
				<tr>
					<th scope="col">If the caller said</th>
					<th scope="col">Then</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td>Nothing</td><td>&ldquo;We did not hear anything,&rdquo; and the call ends.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>&ldquo;Harry&rdquo;, &ldquo;Marv&rdquo;, or &ldquo;Wet Bandit&rdquo;</td><td>Refused, and the call ends.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Anything, with a <code>Confidence</code> above 0 but under 0.5</td><td>Asked to say it again.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>&ldquo;Kate&rdquo;, &ldquo;Mom&rdquo;, &ldquo;Peter&rdquo;, &ldquo;Dad&rdquo;, &ldquo;Buzz&rdquo;, or &ldquo;Frank&rdquo;</td><td>Put through to the house.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>&ldquo;Nero&rdquo; or &ldquo;pizza&rdquo;</td><td>Put through, with a word for the delivery driver.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Anything else</td><td>Asked to leave a message.</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</div>
	<p>The <code>hints</code> attribute lists the names and words the rules look for, which biases recognition toward them. The &ldquo;say it again&rdquo; prompt is a <code>&lt;Gather&gt;</code> with no <code>action</code>, so the second answer comes back to the screening document and goes through the same rules. Some speech engines don&rsquo;t report a confidence and send 0, so 0 isn&rsquo;t treated as unsure; otherwise every caller on those engines would be asked again and again.</p>
</section>

