<section class="section-block">
	<h2>Limits of this design</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>The person answering doesn&rsquo;t hear who is calling.</strong> The rules decide who gets through; the name the caller spoke isn&rsquo;t played to the house phone.</li>
		<li><strong>No caller name lookup.</strong> The VoiceML runtime doesn&rsquo;t send <code>CallerName</code>, so screening uses the caller&rsquo;s number and what they say.</li>
		<li><strong>Caller ID can be faked.</strong> The block and family lists keep out casual nuisance calls, but they aren&rsquo;t security: anyone who sets their caller ID to a family number rings straight through.</li>
		<li><strong>Matching is by keyword.</strong> <code>contains "frank"</code> also matches &ldquo;Franklin&rdquo; and &ldquo;frankly&rdquo;. Choose words your real callers will say and strangers won&rsquo;t.</li>
		<li><strong>The &ldquo;say it again&rdquo; prompt can repeat.</strong> A caller the recognizer stays unsure about is asked again each time; documents can&rsquo;t count attempts.</li>
		<li><strong>Messages stay on your account.</strong> To have them emailed, add the VoiceML Dashboard&rsquo;s voicemail callback to the <code>&lt;Record&gt;</code>, as the <a href="/support/voiceml/build-a-pbx-with-voiceml/">PBX example</a> does.</li>
	</ul>
</section>

